\begin_layout Title
Econometrics I: Quiz 5
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
You wish to test 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\theta)=0$
\end_inset

 in the context in the maximum likelihood estimation.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln L(\theta)$
\end_inset

 be the log-likelihood function, 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\theta)$
\end_inset

 be the gradient vector.
 Explain the ideas of the likelihood ratio test, the Wald test, and the
 efficient score test.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

 be the unrestricted and restricted estimators, respectively.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\series bold
Likelihood Ratio Test (LRT):
\series default
 For this test, we need to know 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 With MLE, we wish to maximize 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln L(\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 For this test, we need to know 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 We know 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln L(\hat{\theta})\geq\ln L(\tilde{\theta})$
\end_inset

.
 If the restrictions are valid, we can expect 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln L(\hat{\theta})-\ln L(\tilde{\theta})\approx0$
\end_inset

.
 If they are not valid, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln L(\hat{\theta})-\ln L(\tilde{\theta})$
\end_inset

 will be very different from zero.
 LRT analyzes the squared Euclidean distance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln L(\hat{\theta})-\ln L(\tilde{\theta})$
\end_inset

 from zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\series bold
Wald Test (WT):
\series default
 We only need information on 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 We want to see if 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 satisfies the restrictions 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\theta)=0$
\end_inset

.
 If the restrictions are valid, then 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\hat{\theta})\approx0$
\end_inset

.
 If they are not valid, then 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\hat{\theta})$
\end_inset

 will be very different from the zero vector.
 The WT analyzes the weighted squared Euclidean distance of 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\hat{\theta})$
\end_inset

 from the zero vector.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\series bold
Efficient Score Test (EST):
\series default
 We only need information on 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 We want to see if 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

 satisfies the FOC of the unrestricted model, 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\theta)=0$
\end_inset

.
 If the restrictions are valid, then 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\tilde{\theta})\approx0$
\end_inset

.
 If the restrictions are not valid, then 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\tilde{\theta})$
\end_inset

 will be very different from the zero vector.
 EST analyzes the weighted squared Euclidean distance of 
\begin_inset Formula $g(\tilde{\theta})$
\end_inset

 from the zero vector.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a standard linear regression model 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=Z_{t}\theta+u_{t}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{t}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $1\times p$
\end_inset

 vector of regressors, and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}\sim i.i.d.(0,\sigma^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 There are 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 variables 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

 that are uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $K\geq p$
\end_inset

.
 You wish to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 by the method of moments.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 is an i.i.d.
 random variable.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Write the population moment condition and the sample moment condition that
 you will use.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Explain the identifiability problem is, and derive the rank condition for
 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 to be identified.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is overidentified by the population moment condition.
 What does that mean?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We discussed an estimation idea to handle the overidentification problem.
 Explain the idea and explain how to choose the seleciton matrix (which
 was denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 in the lecture).
 Present the best 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 and prove that it is indeed the best.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

 be a 
\begin_inset Formula $1\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The population moment condition is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(X_{t}^{\prime}u_{t})=0
\]

\end_inset

and the sample moment condition is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{T}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}X_{t}^{\prime}(y_{t}-Z_{t}\theta)=0
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 is the true value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 Then, it must solve the population moment condition, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}(y_{t}-Z_{t}\theta_{0})\right]=0
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 is identified if and only if
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}(y_{t}-Z_{t}\theta)\right]=0
\]

\end_inset

has a unique solution for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 and it is equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 If there is another vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{1}$
\end_inset

 that also solves the population moment condition, then the true value of
 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is unable to be identified.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}(y_{t}-Z_{t}\theta)\right] & = & E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}(y_{t}-Z_{t}\theta)\right]+E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}(y_{t}-Z_{t}\theta_{0})\right]-E\left[X_{t}^{\prime}(y_{t}-Z_{t}\theta_{0})\right]\\
 & = & 0+E(X_{t}^{\prime}Z_{t})(\theta_{0}-\theta)=0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 is the unique solution if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $rank\ E(X_{t}^{\prime}Z_{t})=0$
\end_inset

.
 This is the rank condition for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 to be identified.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is overidentified by the population moment condition, then the number of
 equations to estimate the regressors is greater than the actual number
 of regressors, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $K>p$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

One way to handle the overidentification problem is to reduce the overidentified
 system to the just-identified system by selecting 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 number of sample moment conditions and then solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 be a 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times p$
\end_inset

 selection matrix with full column rank.
 Premultiplying 
\begin_inset Formula $F^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 to the sample moment conditions, we have the just identified 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 moment conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{m}(\theta)=F^{\prime}\bar{m}(\theta)=\frac{1}{T}(XF)^{\prime}(y-Z\theta)=\frac{1}{T}(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}y-F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z\theta)=0
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z$
\end_inset

 is a square matrix of full rank, the solution to this set of conditions
 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{\theta}=\left(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z\right)^{-1}F^{\prime}X^{\prime}y=\theta+\left(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z\right)^{-1}F^{\prime}X^{\prime}u
\]

\end_inset

with an asymptotic covariance matrix
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta}|X)=\left(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z\right)^{-1}F^{\prime}X^{\prime}\Omega XF\left(Z^{\prime}XF\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

The optimal weightin matrix
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F=\left(X^{\prime}\Omega X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}Z
\]

\end_inset

This choice gives the estimator
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\theta}=\left[Z^{\prime}X\left(X^{\prime}\Omega X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}Z\right]Z^{\prime}X\left(X^{\prime}\Omega X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}y
\]

\end_inset

with the asymptotic covariance matrix
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta}|X)=\left[Z^{\prime}X\left(X^{\prime}\Omega X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}Z\right]^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

The difference in the covariances, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta})-\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta})$
\end_inset

 is postive semidefinite for any choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta})-\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta})$
\end_inset

 is positive semidefinite if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta})\right]^{-1}-\left[\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta})\right]^{-1}$
\end_inset

 is positive semidefinite.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta})\right]^{-1}-\left[\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta})\right]^{-1}=Z^{\prime}X\left(X^{\prime}\Omega X\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}Z-Z^{\prime}XF\left(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}\Omega XF\right)^{-1}F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z
\]

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X^{\prime}\Omega X=D^{\prime}D$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left[\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta})\right]^{-1}-\left[\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta})\right]^{-1} & = & Z^{\prime}X\left(D^{\prime}D\right)^{-1}X^{\prime}Z-Z^{\prime}XF\left(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}\Omega XF\right)^{-1}F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z\\
 & = & Z^{\prime}XD^{-1}D^{\prime-1}X^{\prime}Z-Z^{\prime}XF\left(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}\Omega XF\right)^{-1}F^{\prime}X^{\prime}Z\\
 & = & Z^{\prime}XD^{-1}\left[I_{K}-DF\left(F^{\prime}X^{\prime}\Omega XF\right)^{-1}D^{\prime}F^{\prime}\right]D^{\prime-1}X^{\prime}Z\\
 & = & Z^{\prime}XD^{-1}\left[I_{K}-DF\left(F^{\prime}D^{\prime}DF\right)^{-1}D^{\prime}F^{\prime}\right]D^{\prime-1}X^{\prime}Z
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A=DF$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $B=Z^{\prime}XD^{-1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{A}=I_{K}-A(A^{\prime}A)^{-1}A^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{A}$
\end_inset

 is symmetric and idempotent.
 Thus
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left[\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta})\right]^{-1}-\left[\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta})\right]^{-1} & = & BM_{A}B^{\prime}\\
 & = & BM_{A}M_{A}^{\prime}B^{\prime}\\
 & = & CC^{\prime}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $C=BM_{A}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{cov}(\tilde{\theta})-\mbox{cov}(\hat{\theta})$
\end_inset

 is positive semi-definite.
\end_layout

